\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:future-work}

This work develops a three-dimensional quaternion MEKF in which a latent
Ornstein--Uhlenbeck acceleration prior drives velocity, oscillatory displacement,
and an additional integral-displacement state.  The zero pseudo-measurement on
that integral state provides the low-frequency drift-control mechanism.  The
core estimator and its Live-state stability result are independent of the
particular sea-state scheduler: under the bounded reference geometry,
bounded measurement and pseudo-update gaps, positive covariance bounds,
finite residual-bias correlation time, exogenous bounded parameter schedule,
and no-reset assumptions stated in Sec.~\ref{app:iss-stability}, the local
homogeneous error recursion is uniformly exponentially stable and the nonlinear
remainder gives a local ISS bound.  Startup, magnetic re-gauging, tilt re-lock,
and other hard reconfiguration events remain outside that theorem.

The adaptation layer is deliberately a separate engineering result.  A private
measurement-only Mahony observer supplies levelled vertical acceleration; a
wave-band period estimator and a period-scaled variance estimator generate the
sea-state statistics; and bounded smoothing maps those statistics into the OU
prior and integral regularizer.  OU similarity, periodic-Riccati analysis, and
the reduced drift--distortion model explain the relevant scales and why the
regularizer has a finite optimum in the reduced problem.  They do not determine
the exact optimum of the full coupled MEKF.  The deployed coefficients and the
effective $\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}$ filter-input regularization schedule are
therefore empirical engineering choices tested against the complete estimator.
The channel ablation provides the clearest mechanism result: most of the
measured adaptive benefit is carried by the changing integral-state
regularization, while adapting the directly observed OU acceleration prior with
the regularizer frozen contributes little to the principal vertical score.

Under the paired ten-seed stationary JONSWAP protocol, the seed-wise mean
normalized vertical-displacement error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III and
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II.  The paired difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage point with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
The PM--Stokes ensemble gives the same qualitative vertical result.  With the
current isotropic OU--III configuration, mean 3-D displacement RMS is also lower
than matched OU--II in each of the four stationary JONSWAP seas and in the
controlled transition, with the paired bootstrap interval excluding zero in
each comparison.  That result should still be read within the tested directional and spectral
families rather than as a general absolute-navigation claim.

The horizontal analysis clarifies why isotropic regularization is plausible
even though horizontal low-frequency acceleration error can be roughly two
orders of magnitude larger than the vertical channel.  In the reduced
bias--variance model, drift intensity enters the preferred regularizer only
through a $1/14$ power but enters the minimized residual error through a $4/7$
power.  Thus horizontal performance is more effectively improved by reducing
the disturbance itself---better tilt, accelerometer-bias observability, or an
external horizontal reference---than by simply imposing a much tighter
horizontal $S=0$ constraint.  The measured $x/y$ split remains heading dependent,
so this study supports isotropic regularization for the tested records rather
than a universal pair of world-axis tuning constants.

Several limitations remain.  The inferential comparison is still
primarily against the author-developed OU--II predecessor; the external
nonlinear observer is not run under the same ten-seed protocol, and a
conventional spectral double-integration baseline plus a current published
heave estimator remain needed for a broader ranking.  The changing-sea case is
a controlled crossfade whose blend can be bimodal, not a continuously evolving
directional spectrum.  Temperature is not varied, the optional IMU lever-arm
correction is not physically validated with a known off-center installation,
and the hard-iron experiment does not identify soft-iron or misalignment error.
The ESP32--S3 exercise establishes source portability but not processor, memory,
power, or deadline margins.  Most importantly, the reported displacement is
bounded wave motion about a local reference; no global navigation position is
claimed without external aiding.

\subsection*{Future work}
The most useful extensions follow directly from those limitations and from the
companion designs already present in the repository:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item \textbf{Reference-point and GNSS validation.}  Exercise the implemented
        IMU-to-center-of-gravity lever-arm model on hardware with a surveyed
        nonzero offset, then add the companion GNSS architecture that separates
        slow absolute vessel trajectory from bounded wave motion.  GNSS antenna
        lever arms, asynchronous timestamps, outages, and reacquisition should
        be tested without allowing the integral zero constraint to act on the
        vessel's absolute position.
  \item \textbf{Horizontal observability.}  Quantify how GNSS velocity/position
        aiding, improved tilt information, and stronger accelerometer-bias
        observability reduce the low-frequency horizontal acceleration-error
        spectral density identified in Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}.
        This is a more direct test of the horizontal diagnosis than another
        fixed $R_S$ multiplier sweep.
  \item \textbf{Vessel-response realism.}  Apply vessel response-amplitude
        operators (RAOs) to the incident directional sea before generating IMU
        motion, so the validation distinguishes the ocean spectrum from the
        frequency- and direction-dependent response of a particular hull.
        Include off-CoG rotational acceleration in the same study.
  \item \textbf{Multimodal and multidirectional seas.}  Replace the present
        single-heading stationary families and endpoint crossfade with crossing
        seas, independent swell and wind-sea components, multiple simultaneous
        peaks, heading changes, and genuinely time-varying directional spectra.
        These experiments are especially important for testing the single
        characteristic-period tuner and any future matrix-valued horizontal
        regularizer.
  \item \textbf{Wind-heel separation.}  Test the companion slow-equilibrium
        heel model using apparent wind and vessel motion while retaining full
        wave attitude.  Long swell and asymmetric wave loading must be included
        because a shifted mean roll is not uniquely attributable to wind; wind
        sensor lever-arm motion and gust transients should be modeled rather
        than treated as direct heel measurements.
  \item \textbf{Temperature-dependent bias.}  Replace the fixed-temperature
        simulation with controlled heating/cooling cycles and identify
        calibration error, sensor thermal lag, hysteresis, and the residual
        bias correlation time.  The current \SI{5000}{s} Gauss--Markov residual
        should then be tested against measured temperature histories rather
        than justified only as a bounded stability model.
  \item \textbf{Magnetic calibration under heading excitation.}  Use deliberate
        turns to identify soft-iron and sensor-misalignment terms that the
        present additive hard-iron estimator can alias but cannot distinguish.
  \item \textbf{Independent physical validation and embedded margins.}  Repeat
        the paired protocol against a wave tank, motion platform, optical/GNSS
        displacement reference, or commercial MRU, and measure execution time,
        memory, power, and deadline margin on the embedded target across update
        rates and sensor configurations.
\end{itemize}

